illingness to resort to  to violence not just in Germany but also  abroad in the US of course we had the  ties to the January 6 storming  um Capital riots and um in Germany we  also this is not completely new because  even a few months before the capital  riots uh there was an attempted storming  of the German Parliament and there was a  high involvement of reisberger and of Q  anonent here and there so this doesn't  really come as a surprise but it's still  shocking to see the the large scale of  this of this operation and of of this  plot  now the people involved are said to be  conspiracy theorists to reject the  modern a German state is there an upward  trend of these sentiments in Germany in  general you think  I'm afraid so yes because there is a  very  um unfortunately there is has been now  really a rising anti-establishment  resentment within the German population  that is of course mirrored in other  parts of the world but there is a higher  um there's a higher recruitment  potential for movements like the rice  burger and also Q Anon where we've  really seen these movements expand a lot  in in Germany and Germany was  particularly prone to these conspiracy  myths that are  um of course combining old conspiracy  myths of the reisberger movement and  wanting to re-establish the the  pre-World War II borders and not  believing in the legitimacy of the the  current state and the current democracy  and combining those old older elements  of the reisberger movement with new cure  non-conspiracy myths that then take this  onto a global level where they would  blame the so-called Global Elites and  would also often add anti-anti-semitic  elements to these narratives  now you have researched uh  radicalization on all both ends of the  spectrum if you will so what are the  main reasons for people to become really  radicalized  it's of course usually quite quite  complex and there's not one single  profile for people who become  radicalized towards violence what we'